# SRG-APP-000154-AU-002360  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must be configured to use multifactor
# authentication for network access to privileged accounts such that one of
# the factors is provided by a device separate from the system gaining
# access.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000154-AU-002360
group:           V-206464
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-004046
control_rev5:    IA-2

contribution:    Splunk Web can require a client certificate and derive the user identity
                 from it, so the authenticating factor is held on the PIV or CAC card, a
                 device separate from the machine making the connection, and the OID list
                 tells the product where in the certificate to find the card-borne
                 identifier.

governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           web.conf [settings] requireClientCert equals true
check:           web.conf [settings] enableCertBasedUserAuth equals true
check:           web.conf [settings] certBasedUserAuthMethod in CommonName|EDIPI|PIV
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          web.conf [settings] certBasedUserAuthMethod https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/manage-users-and-security/10.4/authenticate-into-splunk-enterprise-using-hardware/configure-splunk-enterprise-to-use-a-common-access-card-for-authentication
vendor:          web.conf [settings] certBasedUserAuthPivOidList https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/manage-users-and-security/10.4/authenticate-into-splunk-enterprise-using-hardware/configure-splunk-enterprise-to-use-a-common-access-card-for-authentication
vendor:          web.conf [settings] enableCertBasedUserAuth https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/manage-users-and-security/10.4/authenticate-into-splunk-enterprise-using-hardware/configure-splunk-enterprise-to-use-a-common-access-card-for-authentication
vendor:          web.conf [settings] requireClientCert https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/manage-users-and-security/10.4/authenticate-into-splunk-enterprise-using-hardware/configure-splunk-enterprise-to-use-a-common-access-card-for-authentication
retired_check:   web.conf [settings] certBasedUserAuthPivOidList non_empty
                 reason: 10.4.2 spec: "A list of object identifiers (OIDs) that the Splunk
                         platform uses to lookup an end-user's PIV info in the Subject
                         Alternate Name extension of the client certificate... Default:
                         1.3.6.1.4.1.311.20.2.3, Microsoft Universal Principal Name,
                         Microsoft User Principal Name". The CAC page gives the default as
                         "1.3.6.1.4.1.311.20.2.3, Microsoft Universal Principal Name" and
                         says multiple OIDs are comma-separated.
# --- end vendor verification

external:        The PIV or CAC card itself, its issuance under DoD PKI, and the reader and
                 middleware on the client machine, which provide the separate device
external:        The certificate profile that populates the Common Name, EDIPI or PIV OID
                 the application is told to read

citation:        SRG-APP-000154-AU-002360 check and fix text; web.conf.spec:120-132
                 (enableCertBasedUserAuth, default false), web.conf.spec:133-143
                 (certBasedUserAuthMethod: CommonName, EDIPI, PIV; no default),
                 web.conf.spec:144-150 (certBasedUserAuthPivOidList and its default OID
                 set), web.conf.spec:151-167 (requireClientCert, default false)
finding:         Splunk Web is not configured for certificate-based user authentication
                 with an identity extraction method (web.conf requireClientCert,
                 enableCertBasedUserAuth, certBasedUserAuthMethod), so the application does
                 not accept an authentication factor held on a device separate from the
                 connecting system.
